for x in zr:
 v=lambda k:float(x[k]);lines.append('|'+x['m']+'|'+x['q']+'|'+'|'.join(f'{v(k)/v("Weil_T_to_X"):.7g}' for k in ['endpoint_only_T_to_X','edge_remainder_cross_T_to_X','remainder_square_T_to_X','endpoint_error_bound_T_to_X'])+'|')
lines+=['','These finite data do not support discarding the below-cutoff contribution or the rational remainder. The large negative cross term and positive remainder-square term partly cancel; a positive endpoint-square heuristic alone misses that structure. The nonzero head disproves an exact tail-only identification in these finite cells, and the square expansion shows that endpoint-only is not the complete observed finite tail. These findings do **not** refute the empirical bounded-factor prediction R in[3,5], or a future asymptotic law for the selected family. At T=1.1Omega the fixed-m far-frequency expansion is not uniform. The phase-averaged bare endpoint coefficient2 belongs to a fixed-vector far-cutoff limit, not the joint m→infinity limit at T=1.1Omega. It does not by itself contradict coefficient4 in that joint limit; deriving that coefficient requires near-band, phase and head control.','','## DH actual listed quartets through230','','The special source−4nu uses the interpolating normalization F(z0)=i; nu denotes zero multiplicity. Generic ground vectors give4nu(a²−b²), with correction−8nu b² versus ordinary2G. Listed multiplicity1 below is a numerical census convention, not a new simplicity proof.','','|matched case|height|Re F|Im F|actual quartet|correction vs ordinary2G|','|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|']
